\title{Monoidal Strictification of the Parenthesization Category}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{2026-07-12}

\begin{document}

\begin{abstract}
We study a concrete monoidal category built entirely from formal
parenthesizations---the category $\PT(\alpha)$ whose objects are binary trees
with leaves labelled by an alphabet $\alpha$ and whose morphisms are witnesses
that two trees share the same underlying leaf-word. The tensor product is
tree-grafting; it is deliberately \emph{not associative on objects}, and its
associativity is repaired instead by a canonical invertible associator. We show
that $\PT(\alpha)$ is a \emph{thin} category (at most one morphism between any
pair of objects) and observe that on a thin category every coherence law of a
monoidal structure---the pentagon, the triangle, and all naturality squares---
holds automatically. Consequently $\PT(\alpha)$ is a genuine, and genuinely
non-strict, monoidal category. We then construct the \emph{flattening functor}
$\Flat\colon \PT(\alpha)\to\DD(\alpha)$ into the discrete strict monoidal
category of words in the free monoid $\FF(\alpha)$ under concatenation, prove
that it is a strong monoidal functor with identity comparison data, and upgrade
the resulting categorical equivalence $\PT(\alpha)\simeq\DD(\alpha)$ to a
\emph{monoidal equivalence}. This realizes, for this family, the full content of
Mac Lane's strictification theorem. Finally we show that under strictification
the associator is contracted to an identity-type morphism, exhibiting the
``causal loop'' of reassociations collapsing to a point.
\end{abstract}

\maketitle

\section{Introduction}

Associativity is the quiet backbone of algebra. In a set with a binary
operation we typically demand $(a\cdot b)\cdot c = a\cdot(b\cdot c)$ as an
equation, and then drop parentheses forever. In higher-dimensional and
categorical settings, however, the natural objects of study---spaces, systems,
processes---are combined by operations whose associativity holds only \emph{up
to a specified isomorphism}. The two groupings $(a\otimes b)\otimes c$ and
$a\otimes(b\otimes c)$ become distinct objects joined by a chosen comparison
morphism, the \emph{associator}. A \emph{monoidal category} is precisely a
category equipped with such a tensor, a unit, an associator, and unit-law
isomorphisms.

Once associativity is mediated by morphisms rather than equations, one must ask
whether the mediating morphisms are mutually consistent. Mac Lane's
\emph{coherence theorem} answers this: if the associator and unitors satisfy the
\emph{pentagon} and \emph{triangle} diagrams, then every formal diagram built
from them commutes. A sharper structural statement, the \emph{strictification
theorem}, asserts that every monoidal category is monoidally equivalent to a
\emph{strict} one, in which associativity and the unit laws hold on the nose.

This paper is a self-contained development of these ideas for one especially
transparent family: the category of formal parenthesizations. We take the
bracketings themselves as the objects of a category, so that the failure of
strict associativity is visible and honest. We then show that the category is
\emph{thin}, which trivializes all coherence, and carry out strictification
explicitly.

Throughout, $\alpha$ is a fixed type (``alphabet'') of leaf labels, and
$\FF(\alpha)$ denotes the free monoid on $\alpha$: finite words under
concatenation, with empty word $1$ as unit.

\section{Thin categories and free coherence}

\begin{definition}[Thin category]
A category $\mathcal{C}$ is \emph{thin} if for any objects $X,Y$ and any two
morphisms $f,g\colon X\to Y$ we have $f=g$; that is, every hom-set has at most
one element.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Every diagram commutes]\label{lem:comm}
In a thin category, any two parallel composites are equal. In particular any
square, pentagon, or triangle of morphisms commutes automatically.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Two parallel composites are two morphisms with the same source and target; by
thinness they coincide.
\end{proof}

A \emph{monoidal structure} on $\mathcal{C}$ consists of a tensor bifunctor
$\otimes$, a unit object $\unit$, and natural isomorphisms
\[
\alpha_{X,Y,Z}\colon (X\otimes Y)\otimes Z\to X\otimes(Y\otimes Z),\quad
\lambda_X\colon \unit\otimes X\to X,\quad
\rho_X\colon X\otimes\unit\to X,
\]
subject to the pentagon and triangle axioms and naturality. When only the data
are given without the axioms, we call it a \emph{monoidal structure datum}.

\begin{theorem}[Coherence from thinness]\label{thm:coh}
Let $\mathcal{C}$ be a thin category equipped with a monoidal structure datum.
Then all the monoidal axioms---the pentagon identity, the triangle identity,
naturality of the associator and both unitors, and the functoriality laws for
$\otimes$---hold automatically. Hence $\mathcal{C}$ is a genuine monoidal
category.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Each axiom asserts the equality of two parallel morphisms in $\mathcal{C}$. By
Lemma~\ref{lem:comm} every such equality holds.
\end{proof}

\section{The parenthesization category}

\begin{definition}[Trees]
Let $\PT(\alpha)$ be the set of binary trees with leaves labelled by $\alpha$,
generated inductively by: $\nil$ (the empty tree); $\leaf(a)$ for $a\in\alpha$;
and $\node(s,t)$ for trees $s,t$, read as the bracketed product $(s\cdot t)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Flattening]
The \emph{flattening} map $\flat\colon\PT(\alpha)\to\FF(\alpha)$ reads the leaves
left to right:
\[
\flat(\nil)=1,\qquad \flat(\leaf(a))=a,\qquad
\flat(\node(s,t))=\flat(s)\cdot\flat(t).
\]
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[The category $\PT(\alpha)$]
Make $\PT(\alpha)$ a category by declaring $\mathrm{Hom}(s,t)$ to be the set of
proofs that $\flat(s)=\flat(t)$: one element if $\flat(s)=\flat(t)$ and none
otherwise. Identities are reflexivity; composition is transitivity of equality.
\end{definition}

\begin{proposition}[Thin groupoid]\label{prop:thin}
$\PT(\alpha)$ is a thin category, and every morphism is an isomorphism; that is,
$\PT(\alpha)$ is a thin groupoid.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
Two morphisms $s\to t$ are two proofs of the single proposition
$\flat(s)=\flat(t)$; the hom-set is a subsingleton, so they are equal and the
category is thin. A morphism $s\to t$, i.e. $\flat(s)=\flat(t)$, has inverse the
symmetric equality $\flat(t)=\flat(s)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Isomorphisms from word equalities]\label{lem:iso}
For trees $s,t$, any equality $\flat(s)=\flat(t)$ determines an isomorphism
$s\cong t$; by thinness it is the unique isomorphism between $s$ and $t$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{definition}[Monoidal datum on $\PT(\alpha)$]
Set $s\otimes t:=\node(s,t)$ and $\unit:=\nil$. Whiskering is induced by
congruence of multiplication in $\FF(\alpha)$. The comparison isomorphisms are
read off from the free monoid via Lemma~\ref{lem:iso}:
\[
\alpha_{s,t,u}\ \text{from}\ (\flat\,s\cdot\flat\,t)\cdot\flat\,u
= \flat\,s\cdot(\flat\,t\cdot\flat\,u),\quad
\lambda_s\ \text{from}\ 1\cdot\flat\,s=\flat\,s,\quad
\rho_s\ \text{from}\ \flat\,s\cdot 1=\flat\,s.
\]
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[$\PT(\alpha)$ is monoidal]\label{thm:Pmon}
With this datum, $\PT(\alpha)$ is a monoidal category.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
$\PT(\alpha)$ is thin (Proposition~\ref{prop:thin}), so Theorem~\ref{thm:coh}
supplies every coherence axiom.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[Non-strictness]\label{prop:nonstrict}
For beads $a,b,c$, the trees $(a\otimes b)\otimes c=\node(\node(a,b),c)$ and
$a\otimes(b\otimes c)=\node(a,\node(b,c))$ are distinct objects. Hence
$\PT(\alpha)$ is non-strict: associativity fails at the level of objects and is
repaired only by the associator $\alpha_{a,b,c}$, which by Lemma~\ref{lem:iso} is
the unique isomorphism between these objects.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
The two trees have different shapes, hence are unequal as data, though they
flatten to the same word.
\end{proof}

\section{Flattening as a strong monoidal functor}

Let $\DD(\alpha)$ be the \emph{discrete} category on the underlying set of
$\FF(\alpha)$: objects are words, and the only morphisms are identities.
Concatenation makes $\DD(\alpha)$ a \emph{strict} monoidal category, since
concatenation is associative on the nose with empty word as strict unit.

\begin{definition}[Flattening functor]
Define $\Flat\colon\PT(\alpha)\to\DD(\alpha)$ by $\Flat(s):=\flat(s)$ on objects,
sending a morphism $f\colon s\to t$ (which certifies $\flat(s)=\flat(t)$) to the
identity/coincidence morphism of $\DD(\alpha)$. Functoriality is automatic since
$\DD(\alpha)$ is thin.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Strict respect for the monoidal structure]\label{lem:strict}
For all trees $s,t$: $\flat(s\otimes t)=\flat(s)\cdot\flat(t)$ and
$\flat(\unit)=1$, both as literal equalities.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Immediate from the definitions and the choices $s\otimes t=\node(s,t)$,
$\unit=\nil$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Strong monoidality of flattening]\label{thm:strong}
$\Flat$ carries the structure of a strong monoidal functor. The unit comparison
$\varepsilon\colon\unit_{\DD}\to\Flat(\unit)$ and the tensorator
$\mu_{s,t}\colon\Flat(s)\cdot\Flat(t)\to\Flat(s\otimes t)$ are identity
isomorphisms, and all monoidal-functor coherence squares commute automatically.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
By Lemma~\ref{lem:strict} the source and target of $\varepsilon$ and of each
$\mu_{s,t}$ coincide as objects, so both may be taken to be identities. The
naturality and coherence squares are equalities of parallel morphisms in the thin
category $\DD(\alpha)$, hence hold by Lemma~\ref{lem:comm}.
\end{proof}

\section{The monoidal strictification theorem}

\begin{definition}[Right-nested realization]
For a word $w=a_1a_2\cdots a_n$ define its \emph{right-nested bracketing}
\[
\rn(1)=\nil,\qquad \rn(a_1a_2\cdots a_n)=\node(\leaf(a_1),\rn(a_2\cdots a_n)),
\]
inducing a functor $\Rn\colon\DD(\alpha)\to\PT(\alpha)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Round trips]\label{lem:round}
For every word $w$, $\flat(\rn(w))=w$ exactly. For every tree $s$,
$\rn(\flat(s))$ has the same leaf-word as $s$, so there is a canonical (and, by
thinness, unique) isomorphism $s\cong\rn(\flat(s))$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
The first claim is an induction on the length of $w$, using
$\flat(\node(\leaf(a),r))=a\cdot\flat(r)$. The second holds since both $s$ and
$\rn(\flat(s))$ flatten to $\flat(s)$, so Lemma~\ref{lem:iso} applies.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Categorical equivalence]\label{thm:equiv}
The functors $\Flat$ and $\Rn$ form an equivalence of categories
$\PT(\alpha)\simeq\DD(\alpha)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
By Lemma~\ref{lem:round}, $\Flat\circ\Rn=\id_{\DD}$, giving the counit as an
identity natural isomorphism, and $\Rn\circ\Flat$ is naturally isomorphic to
$\id_{\PT}$ via the pointwise isomorphisms $s\cong\rn(\flat(s))$, whose
naturality is automatic by thinness. The triangle identities are equalities of
parallel morphisms in thin categories, hence hold.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Monoidal strictification, this family]\label{thm:main}
The equivalence of Theorem~\ref{thm:equiv} is a monoidal equivalence
$\PT(\alpha)\simeq_{\otimes}\DD(\alpha)$. Its underlying functor $\Flat$ is
strong monoidal (Theorem~\ref{thm:strong}), its inverse $\Rn$ inherits a
canonical strong monoidal structure, and the unit and counit are monoidal natural
isomorphisms. Hence the non-strict parenthesization category $\PT(\alpha)$ is
monoidally equivalent to the strict skeleton $\DD(\alpha)$ of words under
concatenation.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
$\Flat$ is strong monoidal and an equivalence, so its monoidal structure
transports to the inverse: one defines the tensorator and unit comparison of
$\Rn$ from those of $\Flat$ and the (co)unit, and the compatibility conditions
are equalities of parallel morphisms in thin categories, hence hold. The unit and
counit are monoidal because their monoidal-naturality squares live in thin
categories.
\end{proof}

This is a concrete realization of \textbf{Mac Lane's strictification theorem}:
the loop-tolerant $\PT(\alpha)$ is indistinguishable, as a monoidal category,
from the loop-free strict $\DD(\alpha)$.

\section{Contraction of the associator loop}

\begin{theorem}[The loop collapses]\label{thm:collapse}
Under flattening the associator maps to an identity-type morphism: for all trees
$a,b,c$,
\[
\Flat(\alpha_{a,b,c})=\id_{\flat(a)\,\flat(b)\,\flat(c)}
\]
in $\DD(\alpha)$ (the coincidence morphism identifying the two flattened
endpoints, which agree as words).
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The endpoints $(a\otimes b)\otimes c$ and $a\otimes(b\otimes c)$ flatten to the
equal words $(\flat\,a\cdot\flat\,b)\cdot\flat\,c$ and
$\flat\,a\cdot(\flat\,b\cdot\flat\,c)$, equal by associativity in $\FF(\alpha)$.
Thus in the thin category $\DD(\alpha)$ the image of $\alpha_{a,b,c}$ is a
morphism between equal objects, and there is only one such---the identity.
\end{proof}

\section{Algorithms}

\begin{algorithm}[Flatten]
Given a tree, produce its leaf-word by an in-order traversal that concatenates
leaf labels; $\nil$ contributes the empty word. Linear in the number of nodes.
\end{algorithm}

\begin{algorithm}[Right-nested realization]
Given a word $w=a_1\cdots a_n$, build $\rn(w)$ by folding from the right. Linear
in $n$.
\end{algorithm}

\begin{algorithm}[Reassociation morphism]
Given trees $s,t$, decide whether $\flat(s)=\flat(t)$; if so return the unique
reassociation isomorphism $s\cong t$ as the composite
$s\cong\rn(\flat(s))=\rn(\flat(t))\cong t$ through the common right-nested normal
form. This is the concrete content of coherence: any two reassociations of $s$
into $t$ compute the same result.
\end{algorithm}

\section{Applications and discussion}

\textbf{Coherence as a design principle.} Theorem~\ref{thm:coh} isolates thinness
as a sufficient condition for automatic coherence. The family here meets it
because morphisms are ``same-word certificates,'' unique when they exist. This
suggests a general recipe: choose morphisms to be witnesses of an equivalence
relation on objects preserved by the tensor.

\textbf{Normal forms.} The right-nested bracketing is a normal form for objects
up to isomorphism, and Algorithm~7.3 is a decision procedure for canonical
identification. Isomorphism in $\PT(\alpha)$ reduces to word equality---the
combinatorial residue of Mac Lane coherence.

\textbf{Where non-strictness arises.} Monoidal categories model the composition
of processes: tensoring quantum systems, gluing cobordisms, stacking computational
stages, wiring circuits. There the tensor is associative only up to canonical
isomorphism, as here. Strictification (Theorem~\ref{thm:main}) licenses the flat,
bracket-free notations---string diagrams, sequential pipelines---used throughout
these fields.

\textbf{A cautionary boundary.} Coherence is free \emph{because} the category is
thin. Drop thinness and the pentagon can genuinely fail.

\section{Future work}

A braided or symmetric layer over a commutative (multiset) quotient of
$\FF(\alpha)$; a parallel analysis of the unitors and unit coherence in the thin
framework; a bicategorical delooping producing a one-object bicategory with
on-the-nose non-associative horizontal composition; non-thin obstructions where
the pentagon genuinely fails; and the identification of $\PT(\alpha)$ with the
free monoidal category on the discrete category $\alpha$ quotiented by coherence.

\section{Conclusion}

We built a monoidal category out of nothing but parenthesizations, made its
non-associativity honest at the level of objects, and showed that thinness renders
all coherence automatic and forces a monoidal equivalence with a strict,
bracket-free skeleton. The associator, which carried genuine content in the
non-strict world, is contracted to an identity after strictification. The
parenthesization category is a complete, transparent laboratory model of Mac
Lane's coherence and strictification theorems.

\end{document}

